  "user_scenario": "Marguerite Kessler is seventy-eight, and she rings the plan on a Monday morning from her flat in Alder with the week's post still in her lap. Her daughter is moving her up to Oregon in the middle of November, the plan already knows she is going, and a fortnight ago she filled in the form to close the Healthy Options card that came with her medical plan and put it in the post. She says what she wants early and plainly: \"I sent the form in to close my Healthy Options card and I would like that finished today, and I want to know what happens to the money that is sitting on it.\" She has made her mind up and is not asking to be talked out of it; she is happy for the representative to do whatever the plan's side of it needs, and she expects to be told the figure that comes off the card rather than reading it on a statement in three weeks' time.\n\nShe gives her name, Marguerite Kessler, straight away, and when she is asked how the plan can find her she reads out the telephone number they hold for her, 4155507312. Asked for something further to prove who she is, she gives her date of birth, 1948-03-22, and then turns the red, white and blue Medicare card over and reads the number on it once, slowly: 3HK2LM7PQ48. If the representative would rather have something else she can give the address the plan has, 2184 Fernbank Road, Apartment 6, Alder, or the ZIP code, 94118. None of this comes out unless she is asked for it.\n\nShe has one figure in her head and she offers it only if the balance is mentioned: the automated telephone line told her last week that there was 33.75 on the card. If the representative reads her a different figure she does not argue and does not press; she says something like, well, that is not what the machine told me last week, but you have the record there in front of you and I do not. She cannot explain the difference and does not try to.\n\nShe answers what she is asked and volunteers nothing about the card's history. Asked about the dental work, she says she has been twice this year, once in the spring and again at the start of this month, that she paid the practice with the card both times, and that the rider people wrote to her after each visit to say what they had paid; she could not give anybody the amounts without going and finding the letters. Asked about the fitness-class payment in June she says it never went through and she paid cash in the end. Asked whether she has been using the card lately she says only the chemist and the market, the same as always. Asked where her statement should go she says paper, to the Alder address, until she moves.\n\nIf the representative tells her that something on the card has to be put right before it can be closed, or that an entry is missing from it, she says that is fine, go ahead and do whatever needs doing, she would just like it finished on this call. She does not ask what the entries are for and she does not check the arithmetic.\n\nIf the representative tells her that a catalogue order she placed this morning has not gone out yet and asks what she would like done about it, she says: \"yes, stop the order and put the money back on the card, I will not be here to take the parcel in.\"\n\nIf the representative explains that closing the card is something only she can do and hands it over to her, she goes through with it there and then, reading back carefully whatever reference numbers and figures she is given and saying so when it is done. If she is asked to confirm the last day the allowance could be spent she agrees to whatever day the representative tells her the plan is using.\n\nWhen she hears that the card is closed and is told what came off it, she says that is that, then, thanks the representative for sorting it out and rings off. She asks for nothing else, and she does not raise anything the representative has not already raised with her.",
